Start the playout and recording asked for while no sound server answered

Patch 0006 keeps what the voice engine asked of PulseAudio while no server answers, and
Restore starts it again once one does. But InitPlayout and InitRecording failed meanwhile,
so the voice engine never asked to start (AudioState starts only what initialized): a
microphone turned on while the server was away (the voice channel sending again) was only
initialized when the server came back, and recorded nothing until it was turned off and on
again; likewise playout for a call that started meanwhile. Reproduced in the call lab
(tools/call-lab/mic.sh restart:down20 with RESTART_MUTED=1: a's microphone off as the
server goes, turned on while it is away): a did not record within 20 s of the server back.

Without a server they now report success: the voice engine goes on to start, which
StartPlayout and StartRecording keep for the server's return (play_wanted_, rec_wanted_
kStarted), and Restore starts them.

diff --git a/modules/audio_device/linux/audio_device_pulse_linux.cc b/modules/audio_device/linux/audio_device_pulse_linux.cc
index b7244db..42b60a1 100644
--- a/modules/audio_device/linux/audio_device_pulse_linux.cc
+++ b/modules/audio_device/linux/audio_device_pulse_linux.cc
@@ -955,7 +955,10 @@ int32_t AudioDeviceLinuxPulse::InitPlayout() {
   }
   if (!connected_) {
     RTC_LOG(LS_WARNING) << "no PulseAudio server: playout waits for one";
-    return -1;
+    // Fogata, patch 0012: told done, so that the voice engine goes on to start it,
+    // which is kept for the server's return (Restore starts what was started).
+    // Failed, it never asked: what was turned on meanwhile stayed off.
+    return 0;
   }
 
   // Initialize the speaker (devices might have been added or removed)
@@ -1074,7 +1077,10 @@ int32_t AudioDeviceLinuxPulse::InitRecording() {
   }
   if (!connected_) {
     RTC_LOG(LS_WARNING) << "no PulseAudio server: recording waits for one";
-    return -1;
+    // Fogata, patch 0012: told done, so that the voice engine goes on to start it,
+    // which is kept for the server's return (Restore starts what was started).
+    // Failed, it never asked: what was turned on meanwhile stayed off.
+    return 0;
   }
 
   // Initialize the microphone (devices might have been added or removed)
